{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_k5f6quk75l5kotadzhxupq4sswnybdqf6ict3fl7bkgthlwjlpba","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass, and `src/hello.rs` is byte-for-byte unmodified — **the translation was already correct**.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C `.so` exports exactly one symbol, `helloworld`. Symbol diff empty in *both* directions; all Rust imports are libc/libgcc (`NEEDED`: only `libc.so.6`, `libgcc_s.so.1`). No C source was left untranslated — `CMakeLists.txt` compiles one TU, and it has a Rust counterpart.\n- **Phase B** — all 17 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass over seeded-random inputs (~12 000 calls/profile).\n- **Phase C** — all 8 applicable `ERRORS.md` rows pass, each asserting the *same specific* outcome (return value + `ferror` + `errno`), not just \"both failed\".\n- **Every feature combination** — `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]`, so default / `--no-default-features` / `--all-features` are one configuration; all three spellings run green in **both** debug and release (release matters — it sets `panic = \"abort\"`).\n\n## The real findings\n\nThe C is 3 lines with no branches, no parameters, and no error returns, so the risk wasn't a translation bug — it was verification that *looks* green while testing nothing. I found three such traps:\n\n1. **`cargo test` does not build a `cdylib`-only library target.** Only `cargo build` produces `target/<profile>/libhello.so`. My first mutation run had **all 12 mutants survive**, including \"emit nothing at all\" — the tests were loading a stale `.so`. Every earlier green result in this session was meaningless until I caught this. Now guarded by `assert_so_is_fresh` (mtime check → loud `STALE ARTIFACT`) and by `verify.sh` building before every test.\n2. **libtest's own output pollutes an fd-1 capture.** Capturing `helloworld`'s bytes means redirecting the process-wide descriptor, which libtest also writes to. At 8 threads the suite failed spuriously 4–5 tests per run. Fixed with `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` plus `assert_serial_execution`, which refuses to run with an explanation rather than going flaky.\n3. **glibc `setvbuf(fp, NULL, _IOFBF, 0)` does not detach a caller-owned buffer** — `_IO_setvbuf` returns early when the stream already has one. The tiny-buffer row left `stdout` pointing at a freed `Vec`, segfaulting the suite. Teardown now routes through `_IONBF` first.\n\nPlus: `run_captured` wasn't panic-safe, so the first genuine assertion failure had its message written *into the capture file* and vanished. Restoration is now RAII.\n\nTo prove the suite isn't vacuous, `mutation_test.sh` runs 18 mutants: **15 behaviour-changing ones all detected** (including the two that matter most here — writing via Rust's `std::io::stdout` instead of the C `FILE*`, and propagating `printf`'s failure instead of swallowing it as the C does), and **3 observationally-equivalent ones correctly pass**, confirming no false divergences.\n\nOne judgement call worth flagging: `ERRORS.md` documents null-pointer / zero-length / out-of-range-enum as *structurally inapplicable* rather than tested, because `int helloworld();` has no parameters. Instead of skipping that class, I covered its ABI-level equivalent — the unprototyped K&R signature lets a caller legally pass arguments, so E7/E8 push extreme and random values through the argument registers, the stack, and a variadic signature. `c_src/` is untouched (md5s verified).\n\nReproduce with `cd translation && ./verify.sh --with-mutation`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":622,"cache_read_input_tokens":169269,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":622,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b345276c-8cf5-4633-9b9b-53c50a668569","uuid":"07c3ff8f-1cf7-45d3-84c8-3371f7c39ffc","timestamp":"2026-08-28T08:52:14.062Z"}
